### What problem are you trying to solve?

I'm trying to align inputs inside a `<fieldset>` with inputs outside the fieldset. I'm using CSS grid for the outside layout and expected to be able to make the fieldset a subgrid so the columns inherit inside it.

### What solutions exist today?

Replace the `<fieldset>` with `<div role="group" aria-labelledby="id-of-what-would-be-the-legend">`, though this won't have the fieldset's native abilities like the `disabled` attribute. And fieldset is [more accessible](https://adrianroselli.com/2022/07/use-legend-and-fieldset.html#Bugs) than `role="group"`.

### How would you solve it?

Don't require fieldset to "[establish a new block formatting context](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/rendering.html#the-fieldset-and-legend-elements:the-fieldset-element-2)."

To me, visually, a `fieldset` is a normal box with its [rendered `legend`](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/rendering.html#rendered-legend) placed inside its top border. Fieldset has an [anonymous fieldset content box](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/rendering.html#anonymous-fieldset-content-box), though I'm not sure why this is needed. It seems like fieldset should be able to be a grid directly, without the anonymous content box.

### Anything else?

I made [test cases](https://codepen.io/kirk-l/pen/LEpjXZP?editors=1100) to demonstrate two issues I ran into when trying to make fieldset a subgrid. The first was that Chromium does not allow fieldset to be a subgrid at all.[^1] The second was that while Firefox allows fieldset to be a subgrid, the fieldset is too wide when it has a border. In both cases, changing the `<fieldset>` to a `<div>` rendered as I wanted.

[^1]: A Chromium bug report ([CSS subgrid does not work on nested fieldset elements](https://issues.chromium.org/issues/40278914#comment5)) made me realize that the spec doesn't allow fieldset to be a subgrid because its `<display-inside>` value is `flow-root`.

<details>
<summary>Test cases code</summary>

```html
<!-- Chromium: fieldset not allowed to be subgrid -->
<div class="grid">
  <fieldset class="fieldset1">
    <legend class="legend"></legend>
    <div class="item1"></div>
    <div class="item2"></div>
  </fieldset>
</div>

<!-- Firefox border bug when fieldset is subgrid -->
<div class="grid">
  <fieldset class="fieldset2">
  </fieldset>
</div>

<style>
* {
  box-sizing: border-box;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
}

.grid {
  margin: 20px;
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 70px 30px;
  width: 100px;
  height: 100px;
  background: red;
}

.fieldset1 {
  grid-column: 1 / -1;
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: subgrid;
  border: 0;
}

.fieldset2 {
  grid-column: 1 / -1;
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: subgrid;
  border: 5px solid green;
  background: green;
}

.legend {
  grid-column: 1 / -1;
  /* float prevents legend from being a rendered legend
     https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/rendering.html#rendered-legend */
  float: left;
  background: green;
}

.item1 {
  background: green;
  grid-column: 2;
}

.item2 {
  background: green;
  grid-column: 1;
  grid-row: 2;
}
</style>
```

</details>
